WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.154
An industrial control system, or ICS, is a combination of hardware, software, networks, procedures, and people used to monitor and control an industrial process.

00:00:11.154 --> 00:00:19.684
It turns measurements from the physical world into information, applies control logic, and sends actions back to equipment.

00:00:19.684 --> 00:00:24.277
The purpose is reliable operation, not merely data processing.

00:00:24.277 --> 00:00:28.870
ICS is closely related to operational technology, or OT.

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:34.776
OT is the broader category of technology that interacts with physical operations.

00:00:34.776 --> 00:00:42.431
Industrial control systems are a major part of OT, although organizations may use the terms differently.

00:00:42.431 --> 00:00:50.961
What matters is the operational role: a system may affect production, movement, energy, water, environmental conditions, or safety.

00:00:50.961 --> 00:00:59.928
Examples include manufacturing lines, electrical generation and distribution, water treatment and pumping, pipeline operations, transportation signaling, and facility automation.

00:00:59.928 --> 00:01:08.020
The scale varies from a local machine controller to a supervisory platform coordinating hundreds of remote sites.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:11.739
A basic control loop illustrates the relationship.

00:01:11.739 --> 00:01:16.769
A sensor measures a condition such as pressure or position.

00:01:16.769 --> 00:01:22.237
A controller compares that input with programmed logic or a target.

00:01:22.237 --> 00:01:27.705
An actuator changes equipment, and the process produces a new measurement.

00:01:27.705 --> 00:01:33.610
Operators supervise this loop through displays, alarms, trends, and approved manual controls.

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:37.766
ICS security requires understanding both technology and process.

00:01:37.766 --> 00:01:41.046
A server outage may remove visibility.

00:01:41.046 --> 00:01:44.764
A controller change may alter product quality.

00:01:44.764 --> 00:01:50.232
A communications failure may interrupt control or force a safe shutdown.

00:01:50.232 --> 00:01:55.700
The consequence depends on architecture, equipment state, redundancy, and operating procedures.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.981
Defenders therefore protect more than computers.

00:01:58.981 --> 00:02:03.137
They protect safety, reliability, availability, and process integrity.

00:02:03.137 --> 00:02:13.854
Effective decisions come from combining cybersecurity evidence with engineering knowledge, operating context, and clear authority for actions that could affect the physical system.
